{"id": "2026-08-10-sports-vollering-wins-second-tour-de-france-femmes", "date": "2026-08-10", "stream": "sports", "headline": "Vollering wins second Tour de France Femmes", "summary": "Demi Vollering soloed to the final stage in Nice to win the 2026 Tour de France Femmes for a second time; Swiss rider Marlen Reusser crashed on the last stage and slid to 14th overall.", "url": "https://www.letourfemmes.fr/en/rankings", "source_domain": "letourfemmes.fr", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["[new source]"], "topics": ["sports"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "The race that opened on Swiss roads a week earlier ended with a show of force. Dutchwoman Demi Vollering (FDJ United–SUEZ) attacked 16 km from the finish of the closing stage in Nice, dropped her main rival and rode in alone to seal the overall title — the biggest prize in women's cycling — for the second time after 2023. Poland's Katarzyna Niewiadoma-Phinney held on to second at 1 minute 18 seconds, with Italy's Elisa Longo Borghini third. The Swiss story was a painful one: Marlen Reusser (Movistar) — one of the world's best riders against the clock, and the winner of this Tour's individual time trial — crashed roughly 60 km from the line on the final stage and, though she remounted, slid to 14th overall, her podium hopes gone in an instant. Compatriot Noemi Rüegg, fourth after the opening weekend, faded out of the top ten as the mountains bit.", "why": "A dominant winner and a cruel Swiss near-miss in the same afternoon — Reusser had ridden herself into podium contention before a single crash on the last day undid nine stages of work, the kind of fine margin that defines stage racing.", "thread_id": "2026-08-10-sports-vollering-wins-second-tour-de-france-femmes", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-10", "event_date": "2026-08-09", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Tour de France Femmes", "Demi Vollering", "Marlen Reusser", "Katarzyna Niewiadoma"]}
{"id": "2026-08-10-sports-lugano-lead-super-league-after-three-rounds", "date": "2026-08-10", "stream": "sports", "headline": "Lugano lead Super League after three rounds", "summary": "FC Lugano top the Swiss Super League with the only perfect record after three rounds, beating Zurich 2-0; Basel beat Thun 4-2 and Young Boys drew 2-2 at Lausanne.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/sport/fussball/super-league/super-league-3-runde-fcz-bleibt-chancenlos-lugano-siegt-und-siegt-weiter", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["sports"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Three rounds into the Swiss season and the pacesetter is not one of the country's traditional heavyweights but FC Lugano, the club from Italian-speaking Ticino. A 2-0 home win over FC Zürich — a Kevin Behrens double inside 18 first-half minutes — left Lugano the only side with a perfect nine points and a 11-2 goal difference, and unbeaten across six competitive matches once European qualifiers are counted. Behind them the picture shuffled: record champions Young Boys, who had scored 10 goals in their first two games, threw away a 2-0 lead to draw 2-2 at Lausanne after Edimilson Fernandes was sent off just before the hour; Basel, beaten on the opening weekends, steadied themselves by beating last season's surprise champions Thun 4-2, with Djibril Sow scoring twice and setting up two more. It remains far too early to call a title race in a league that runs to next May, but the early order has again been inverted — the biggest names chasing a side most had pencilled in, at best, as outside contenders.", "why": "In a league usually carved up between Young Boys, Basel and a few others, the team sitting top after a month is a modestly-resourced Ticino club playing the most convincing football in the country — a reminder that the Swiss top flight is more open than its budgets suggest.", "thread_id": "2026-08-03-sports-young-boys-and-lugano-lead-super-league", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-03", "event_date": "2026-08-09", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["FC Lugano", "Swiss Super League", "BSC Young Boys", "FC Basel"]}
{"id": "2026-08-10-sports-guimaraes-joins-arsenal-from-newcastle", "date": "2026-08-10", "stream": "sports", "headline": "Guimaraes joins Arsenal from Newcastle", "summary": "Arsenal signed Brazil midfielder Bruno Guimaraes from rival Newcastle for a reported €87m, their second-most-expensive signing ever, on a long-term deal.", "url": "https://www.arsenal.com/news/bruno-guimaraes-joins-arsenal-assZJ0i5HUEi", "source_domain": "arsenal.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["[new source]"], "topics": ["sports"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Arsenal — last season's English champions — signed Brazil midfielder Bruno Guimaraes from domestic rival Newcastle United, the club confirming a long-term deal on Saturday. At a reported €87 million, the 28-year-old becomes Arsenal's second-most-expensive signing in their history, behind only Declan Rice (€117m from West Ham in 2023), and leaves Newcastle after four-and-a-half seasons and nearly 200 appearances. It is the marquee move of an English window that does not close until early September, and a statement of intent from a club that came close but has not won the Premier League in over two decades. (Elsewhere the window kept turning: France left-back Lucas Digne returned to Paris Saint-Germain from Aston Villa 11 years after leaving, and defender Trevoh Chalobah left Chelsea for Italy's Como.)", "why": "Prising a key player away from a direct rival, at close to a club-record fee, is the kind of aggressive spending that signals Arsenal think this is the season they finally convert near-misses into a title.", "thread_id": "2026-08-10-sports-guimaraes-joins-arsenal-from-newcastle", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-10", "event_date": "2026-08-08", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Arsenal", "Bruno Guimaraes", "Newcastle United"]}
{"id": "2026-08-10-sports-bencic-reaches-toronto-quarter-final", "date": "2026-08-10", "stream": "sports", "headline": "Bencic reaches Toronto quarter-final", "summary": "Belinda Bencic reached the Canadian Open (WTA 1000) quarter-finals with a 6-4 6-0 win over Alexandra Eala, while top men Sinner and Alcaraz sit out the US Open lead-up.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/sport/tennis/wta-tour/6-4-6-0-in-toronto-bencic-fegt-ueberfliegerin-eala-vom-platz", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["[single-source]"], "topics": ["sports"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Belinda Bencic, the Swiss No. 1 and Olympic champion, reached the quarter-finals of the Canadian Open — one of the biggest tournaments below the four Grand Slams (a WTA 1000, the women's tour's top tier) — with a commanding 6-4, 6-0 win over Alexandra Eala. It was a statement result: the 21-year-old from the Philippines had reached the Wimbledon fourth round and won her first tour title in Washington a week ago, and Bencic ended a seven-match winning run in 73 minutes. It is Bencic's second WTA 1000 quarter-final of the year, and her next opponent is again world No. 2 Coco Gauff, as in Miami in the spring. The tournament is being reshaped by absences at the top of the men's game running in parallel: world No. 1 Jannik Sinner has withdrawn from next week's Cincinnati Open, while defending Grand Slam contender Carlos Alcaraz is out injured with a lingering wrist problem — thinning the fields at the last big events before the US Open, which begins in late August.", "why": "A deep run for the Swiss No. 1 at a top-tier event is exactly the form that matters heading into the US Open — and with the two best men in the sport skipping or missing the American swing, the hard-court lead-up is suddenly wide open.", "thread_id": "2026-08-10-sports-bencic-reaches-toronto-quarter-final", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-10", "event_date": "2026-08-09", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Belinda Bencic", "Canadian Open", "Alexandra Eala", "Coco Gauff"]}
